<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-align-center"><strong>Underrepresentation of Non-Native English Speakers in Analytic Philosophy: Findings from over 100,000 Papers</strong><br><em>by Neri Marsili and Paul Rehren</em></h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It’s no secret that a good command of English is a prerequisite for a successful career in analytic philosophy. But what if English isn’t your first language? As many non-native English speakers (&#8220;non-natives&#8221;, for short) can attest, having learnt English as a second language can create friction at every stage of your academic career, from your first days as a student to tenure and beyond. Yet while obstacles tied to <a href="https://dailynous.com/2021/11/16/women-in-philosophy-recent-reports/">gender</a> or <a href="https://www.philosophy.rutgers.edu/images/archive/what_is_the_state_of_blacks_in_philosophy-1.pdf">race</a> have long been recognised in the profession (and rightly so), disadvantages due to native language have only recently begun to attract attention and research.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So, for example, a recent <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s13164-025-00779-8">survey of more than 1,600 philosophers</a> (discussed on <a href="https://dailynous.com/2025/09/24/the-hidden-costs-of-being-a-non-native-english-speaker-in-philosophy-guest-post/">this very blog</a> last year) found that non-native English speaking scholars on average need twice as long to read a paper in English and nearly twice as long to prepare a talk (even controlling for proficiency). The hurdles, however, don’t stop at manuscript preparation. Getting published isn’t easy either: non-native authors only accounted for <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10670-018-9989-4">31% of the authors in 18 philosophy journals</a> between 2013 and 2017&#8212;not much, considering philosophy is done all over the world. Those who are lucky enough to get published still get comparatively little attention. Among the <a href="https://yetanotherphilosophersblog.blogspot.com/2014/09/analytic-philosophy-and-english.html">500 most cited works</a> in four leading generalist journals, only 5.8% were by non-native philosophers.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While this research offers valuable insights, it has important limitations&#8212;perhaps inevitably so, given how new this area of study still is. The papers and blog posts just cited looked at relatively narrow samples, covering relatively short periods of time; they reported only raw percentages (which say little about representation) and did little to pin down what causes the gap between native and non-native speakers.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading">Our Study</h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">At a workshop on corpus methods back in 2021, it struck me (Neri) that most of these limitations could be overcome by studying the problem at scale. Paul Rehren turned out to have exactly the data we needed. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1186/s41073-026-00252-9" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Our study</a>, just published in <em>Research Integrity and Peer Review</em>, covers over 100,000 English-language articles published in more than 200 philosophy journals between 1975 and 2021.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Here’s how we did it. Since we obviously couldn’t check every author’s native language one by one, we used two proxies: whether the author works in an English-speaking country, and whether they got their PhD in one. This method is inexact, of course. Still, when we checked a random sample by hand, both proxies were right about nine times out of ten: imprecise, but not too bad.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Next, we needed a baseline: to quantify underrepresentation, we needed to establish the percentage of non-native philosophers in the profession. To estimate this, we turned to <a href="https://philpeople.org/">PhilPeople</a>. Using the country for current academic staff as a proxy, we estimated this share at just over half (53%). We suspect, however, that the real share is substantially higher, since PhilPeople, <a href="https://philpeople.org/help">by its own account</a>, has “heavier coverage in Anglophone countries”. This matters for what you’re about to read: our estimates of underrepresentation are likely very conservative.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What does the data say? Overall, non-native authors come fairly close to “parity”&#8212;having a share of publications proportional to their share of the professional population&#8212;on one proxy (where they work, about 9/10 of what one would expect if they were equally well represented), and less close on the other (where they got their PhD, just over half). Before concluding that their underrepresentation is overstated, though, consider what this means. Nobody ever suggested that non-native philosophers struggle to publish <em>anywhere</em>. Surely you can place a paper in the least selective journal of your discipline even if your English prose doesn’t display the “<a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20211202215859/https:/aap.org.au/instructions">flair, elegance, or vivacity</a>” of a native speaker. What matters, by any reasonable standard, are the journals at the top: these are the publications that get you a decent readership, and that matter for hiring, funding and professional recognition.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We defined <em>top journals</em> in two ways: broadly, as the thirty or so journals ranked in the Leiter Reports polls, and narrowly, as the “Big Four”: <em>Philosophical Review</em>, <em>Mind</em>, <em>Noûs</em> and <em>Journal of Philosophy</em>. Once we focus on these subsets, the picture changes. In the broader group, non-native authors publish either <em>half as much</em> or <em>a quarter as much</em> as their numbers would predict (depending on the proxy, see Figure 2). In the Big Four, they publish between 12% and 38% of what you would expect. (We also looked at a third definition of top journals based on citation impact, which produced similar results).</p>



