<p class="wp-block-paragraph">One cool feature of our dataset is that it covers almost five decades, so we can look at how differences evolve over time. Things are improving, but it doesn’t look great where it matters. Since 1975, representation in lower-ranked journals has improved a lot, to the point of near parity (classifying authors by where they work). Representation in the top journals has improved, too, but that change has been quite a bit slower. As a result, since 1975 the gap between the top and bottom journals has become much wider.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="999" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fig-2-revised-1024x999.png" alt="" class="wp-image-27317" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fig-2-revised-1024x999.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fig-2-revised-300x293.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fig-2-revised-768x749.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fig-2-revised-250x244.png 250w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/fig-2-revised.png 1308w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Figure 2. Non-native English-speaker representation by year, classified by where authors work. The dashed line is parity. Source: Marsili and Rehren (2026).</figcaption></figure>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading">What explains the gap?</h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With our data, we could test three potential explanations for the gap between top journals and other journals.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>first hypothesis</strong> is a familiar story, which some readers of this blog have suggested in other discussions as an innocent explanation of the gap. Maybe non-native authors simply write less polished English, and top journals are pickier about style. If so, the gap should be larger for authors whose first language is very different from English and who consequently have a harder time learning it, such as Japanese or Korean, and smaller for closer languages like Dutch. There is a slight trend in that direction, but it is weak, and it is driven almost entirely by a few outliers closest to English (mostly Norwegian and Swedish authors). Language quality doesn’t seem to be a major driver of underrepresentation, at least by our (admittedly crude) measure of linguistic distance.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>second hypothesis</strong> is that <em>non-native authors face active discrimination</em> from reviewers. We tested this through a natural experiment. Over the past few decades, some top journals switched from open or single-anonymous review to double- or triple-anonymous review. If reviewers were penalizing authors for their non-native background, non-native representation should go up after the switch. Which is exactly what we found. Classified by where authors work, representation jumped from around a fifth to around a third of what parity requires. Classified by PhD institution, the change, though also positive, is much smaller and statistically uncertain. (On the assumption of active discrimination, this pattern would be somewhat unsurprising, since unblinded manuscripts show authors’ affiliations, but not where authors got their PhDs.)</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Let’s keep in mind, too, that anonymous review hides a bunch of things at once: the author’s name, their institution, its prestige, the country where it’s based. To call this discrimination <em>against non-natives</em> (as opposed to discrimination that affects non-natives) might be a bit of a stretch, as the effect might be driven by a few compounding factors (we discuss more limitations in the paper). Still, what we detected is certainly consistent with some form of discrimination.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="501" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-1024x501.png" alt="" class="wp-image-27315" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-1024x501.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-300x147.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-768x375.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-1536x751.png 1536w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-2048x1001.png 2048w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Fig3_before_after_anonymous_review-250x122.png 250w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Figure 3. Non-native English speaker representation before and after the switch to anonymous review (plotted as year 0). </figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <strong>third hypothesis</strong> is that <em>non-native English speakers simply have different interests</em>. Top journals are quite particular about the topics they deem worthy of their attention: <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/409230393_Are_Philosophy%27s_Top_Journals_Out_of_Step_New_Data_on_Demographic_and_Sub-Field_Representation_in_a_Large_Sample_of_Philosophy_Journals">they publish mostly metaphysics and epistemology</a>, and very little from what PhilPapers calls “philosophical traditions” (continental and non-Western philosophy, for instance). Perhaps this mix of subfields caters better to the interests of native authors than to those of non-natives, and this contributes to the gap. According to our data, this conjecture is not far off: the work of non-native authors is comparatively more concentrated in the areas top journals publish least, and less concentrated in the areas they publish most.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It would be naive, however, to read this as an indication that non-native authors and their idiosyncratic interests are to blame for the gap. What a journal publishes is in large part an editorial choice. What seems like poor strategy from the native’s side might equally be read as self-serving topic selection by editorial boards whose interests reflect the Anglophone institutions in which they are based.</p>



<h4 class="wp-block-heading">What this Shows and What Can Be Done</h4>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Our study has significant limitations. Our proxies are noisy. We have no data on books. Above all, we don’t have submission data, so we can’t measure how much of the gap comes from non-native philosophers submitting fewer papers to top journals. Even so, this is the largest and most systematic study of linguistic inequity in academic philosophy to date. With all its limitations, it confirms the picture painted by earlier work: non-native philosophers are underrepresented in the journals that matter most, and the gap is getting wider.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What can be done to improve the situation? Our data suggest that increasing anonymity on top journals that haven’t yet transitioned to triple blind could help. More anonymity would be a good idea anyway, given our profession’s problem with <a href="https://doi.org/10.3998/ergo.12405314.0005.010">prestige bias</a>. Top journals could also appoint more people from outside the English-speaking world to their editorial boards: currently, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/05568641.2018.1429741">only about 4% of the editors</a> of fifteen prominent journals work at institutions outside Anglophone countries. If boards better reflected the actual composition of the profession, there’s a chance that their editorial choices (which subfields they focus on, which level of English they deem stylistically acceptable) might too, giving non-native scholars a fairer shot at publishing. Finally, the <a href="https://biap.ub.edu/bp/">Barcelona Principles for a Globally Inclusive Philosophy</a> ask very little of journals and institutions: it wouldn’t hurt if more journals signed up. We’d get an extra perk, too: since the Principles recommend collecting statistics on submissions by non-native speakers, we’d finally get the data we need to settle some of the questions that our study left open.</p>
